DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       bbaacckkttrraaccee()  returns  a backtrace for the calling program, in the array
       pointed to by _b_u_f_f_e_r.  A backtrace is the series  of  currently  active
       function  calls  for the program.  Each item in the array pointed to by
       _b_u_f_f_e_r is of type _v_o_i_d _*, and is the return  address  from  the  corre‐
       sponding  stack  frame.  The _s_i_z_e argument specifies the maximum number
       of addresses that can be stored in _b_u_f_f_e_r.  If the backtrace is  larger
       than  _s_i_z_e,  then  the  addresses corresponding to the _s_i_z_e most recent
       function calls are returned; to obtain  the  complete  backtrace,  make
       sure that _b_u_f_f_e_r and _s_i_z_e are large enough.

       Given  the  set  of  addresses returned by bbaacckkttrraaccee() in _b_u_f_f_e_r, bbaacckk‐‐
       ttrraaccee__ssyymmbboollss() translates the addresses into an array of strings  that
       describe  the  addresses symbolically.  The _s_i_z_e argument specifies the
       number of addresses in _b_u_f_f_e_r.  The symbolic representation of each ad‐
       dress consists of the function name (if  this  can  be  determined),  a
       hexadecimal offset into the function, and the actual return address (in
       hexadecimal).   The address of the array of string pointers is returned
       as the function result of  bbaacckkttrraaccee__ssyymmbboollss().   This  array  is  mmaall‐‐
       lloocc(3)ed by bbaacckkttrraaccee__ssyymmbboollss(), and must be freed by the caller.  (The
       strings  pointed to by the array of pointers need not and should not be
       freed.)

       bbaacckkttrraaccee__ssyymmbboollss__ffdd() takes the same  _b_u_f_f_e_r  and  _s_i_z_e  arguments  as
       bbaacckkttrraaccee__ssyymmbboollss(),  but  instead  of returning an array of strings to
       the caller, it writes the strings, one per line, to the file descriptor
       _f_d.  bbaacckkttrraaccee__ssyymmbboollss__ffdd() does not call mmaalllloocc(3), and so can be  em‐
       ployed  in  situations  where  the  latter function might fail, but see
       NOTES.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       bbaacckkttrraaccee() returns the number of addresses returned in  _b_u_f_f_e_r,  which
       is  not greater than _s_i_z_e.  If the return value is less than _s_i_z_e, then
       the full backtrace was stored; if it is equal to _s_i_z_e, then it may have
       been truncated, in which case, the addresses of the oldest stack frames
       are not returned.

       On success, bbaacckkttrraaccee__ssyymmbboollss() returns a pointer  to  the  array  mmaall‐‐
       lloocc(3)ed by the call; on error, NULL is returned.

